{"name":"Muscovite","hex":"#e9decb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Muscovite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inhabitant or native of Muscovy or Moscow.","A Russian."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to Muscovy or Moscow, or the people of these places.","Russian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Muscovite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Muscovite","blocks":[{"text":"Muscovite (also known as common mica, isinglass, or potash mica) is a hydrated phyllosilicate mineral of aluminium and potassium with formula KAl2(AlSi3O10)(OH)2. It has a highly perfect basal cleavage yielding remarkably thin laminae (sheets) which are often highly elastic. Sheets of muscovite 5 by 3 meters (16.4 ft × 9.8 ft) have been found in Nellore, India."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Muscovite-Albite-122887.jpg/330px-Muscovite-Albite-122887.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscovite","wikidata":"Q106532"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Muscovite_window.jpg/500px-Muscovite_window.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Muscovite_window.jpg/1280px-Muscovite_window.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Muscovite_window.jpg/500px-Muscovite_window.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Muscovite window","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuscovite_window.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/120px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/250px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en